      <!-- Section 1: Observed Changes -->
      <section class="content-section" id="observed-changes" aria-label="Observed changes and current state">
        <h3>Observed Changes and Current State</h3>
        <div class="section-body">
          <div class="text-col">
            <p>Global surface temperature has risen <span class="highlight-stat">1.1°C</span> above pre-industrial levels (1850–1900). Atmospheric CO₂ reached <span class="highlight-stat">410 ppm</span> in 2019 — the highest concentration in at least 2 million years. Global sea level rose approximately <span class="highlight-stat">0.20 m</span> between 1901 and 2018, and Arctic sea ice extent in September declined by <span class="highlight-stat">~40%</span> since 1979.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="text-col">
            <p>Every region on Earth has experienced increases in the frequency and intensity of <span class="highlight-warn">extreme weather events</span> — heatwaves, heavy precipitation, droughts, and tropical cyclones — with clear attribution to human influence. These changes are unprecedented over many centuries to millennia.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Future Projections / SSP Scenarios -->
      <section class="content-section" id="future-projections" aria-label="Future projections under SSP scenarios">
        <h3>Future Projections Under Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs)</h3>
        <div class="section-body">
          <div class="text-col" style="flex: 0.85;">
            <p>Global warming projections diverge dramatically depending on emissions pathways. Under the <span class="highlight-stat">very low emissions scenario (SSP1-1.9)</span>, warming is likely to reach 1.5°C near-term before declining slightly to ~1.4°C by 2100. Under <span class="highlight-danger">very high emissions (SSP5-8.5)</span>, warming could exceed <span class="highlight-danger">4.4°C by 2100</span>.</p>
            <p style="margin-top: 8px;">Sea level is projected to rise <span class="highlight-stat">0.28–0.55 m</span> (SSP1-1.9) to <span class="highlight-danger">0.63–1.01 m</span> (SSP5-8.5) by 2100, with continued rise for centuries regardless of pathway.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="chart-area">
            <div class="chart-container" style="height: 280px; background: var(--surface); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px;">
              <canvas id="sspChart" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart showing projected global warming by 2100 across five SSP scenarios, ranging from 1.4°C under SSP1-1.9 to 4.4°C under SSP5-8.5"></canvas>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Risks and Impacts -->
      <section class="content-section" id="risks-and-impacts" aria-label="Risks vulnerabilities and cascading impacts">
        <h3>Risks, Vulnerabilities, and Cascading Impacts</h3>
        <div class="risks-grid">
          <div class="risk-item"><strong>3.3–3.6 billion</strong> people live in highly vulnerable contexts. Climate hazards increasingly interact with <strong>poverty, inequality, and ecosystem degradation</strong> to produce cascading and compounding risks.</div>
          <div class="risk-item"><strong>Biodiversity:</strong> Up to 14% of terrestrial species face very high extinction risk at 1.5°C, rising sharply at higher warming levels. Ecosystem collapse risks accelerate beyond 1.5°C.</div>
          <div class="risk-item"><strong>Food &amp; Water:</strong> Crop yield growth has slowed across regions. Fisheries productivity declining in tropical zones. Over half the world's population faces severe water scarcity at least one month per year.</div>
